{
  "id": "physics-006",
  "title": "How do airplanes stay in the air?",
  "category": "physics",
  "subject_area": "forces and flight",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "How can a heavy airplane stay up in the air, and what do its engines really do?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "An airplane flies when the lift made by its wings balances its weight, and its engines supply thrust that balances the drag.",
  "domain_of_validity": "An airplane in steady, level cruising flight; the forces change when the pilot changes the throttle or the angle of the wings.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "Four forces act on a flying airplane. Weight pulls it down, and lift from the wings pushes it up. Drag from the air slows it down, and thrust from the engines pushes it forward. When the forces balance, the airplane flies along at a steady speed. The engines do not hold the plane up. The wings do.",
    "level_2": "Lift is made by the motion of the airplane through the air, mostly by the wings, and it points perpendicular to the direction of flight. Weight pulls toward the centre of Earth. Drag opposes the motion, and thrust, made by the engines, overcomes it. If the forces are balanced, the airplane cruises at constant velocity. If they are unbalanced, the airplane accelerates in the direction of the largest force.",
    "level_3": "In steady cruise there is no net force, so by Newton's first law the velocity stays constant: lift equals weight, L = W, and thrust equals drag, T = D. Weight depends on the mass of the aircraft, its fuel and its payload, and it falls as fuel is burned, but in cruise the change is small. If the pilot changes the throttle or the angle of the wings, the forces become unbalanced, and Newton's second law gives the acceleration."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "A cruising airliner is the closest real example of balanced forces.",
    "Gliders have no engines at all and still fly well, because their wings make the lift."
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
